Conclusions
The overall impact composting on GHG emissions is very small. The method of composting
does impact GHG emissions, where GHG emissions are higher from outdoor windrow
composting systems than from controlled aerated systems. Further research is required to assess
the whole manure management system, but composting appears to have great potential to reduce
GHG emissions from agriculture. The additional advantage is that composting also addresses a
number of other environmental concerns such as pathogens, surface and groundwater quality and
ammonia emissions. The composting process does not require as much capital investment as
some of the other organic waste solutions.
